+- **Degenerate Input:** If the stereo depth is uniformly corrupt, the underfitting depth may also be poor. The method assumes a mix of valid and corrupt depth [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Perceptual Distillation
-A technique that transfers deblurred texture information from pre-deblurred images to the NeRF using perceptual loss on rendered patches, overcoming geometric inconsistency of pre-deblurred images [S2]. It uses an off-the-shelf deep learning-based image deblurring network $\Theta_D$ to generate pre-deblurred images $\bar{I}$ from blurred inputs $I$ ($\bar{I} = \Theta_D(I)$) [S2]. Perceptual loss is applied by extracting features from both the pre-deblurred color patch $\bar{C}_{ptc}$ and the rendered color patch $\hat{C}_{ptc}$ (rendered from patch-wise sampled rays $r_{ptc}^{pd}$) using a shared pre-trained image feature extractor $\phi$ [S2]. A key pitfall is that pre-deblurred images cannot be used as direct pixel-wise color supervision due to lack of 3D consistency caused by the ill-posed property of image deblurring and independent processing across multi-view images [S2].
+A technique that transfers deblurred texture information from pre-deblurred images to the NeRF using perceptual loss on rendered patches, overcoming geometric inconsistency of pre-deblurred images [S2]. It uses an off-the-shelf deep learning-based image deblurring network to generate pre-deblurred images from blurred inputs [S2]. Rendered color patches $\hat{C}_{ptc}$ are generated from patch-wise sampled rays $r_{ptc}^{pd}$ at the same pixel locations as the pre-deblurred color patches $\bar{C}_{ptc}$ [S2]. Perceptual loss is applied by extracting features from both patches using a shared pre-trained image feature extractor [S2]. Pre-deblurred images cannot be used as direct pixel-wise color supervision due to lack of 3D consistency caused by the ill-posed property of image deblurring and independent processing across multi-view images [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Adds negligible overhead during training, primarily the cost of rendering an additional depth map at iteration $K$ and computing residuals. The patching operation is $O(HW)$ [S1].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the underfitting depth map $D_b^K_i$ temporarily, $O(HW)$ [S1].
-- **Measured Performance:** Evaluated on robotic surgery stereo videos (DaVinci prostatectomy data, 807 frames, 15fps) [S1]. Metrics include PSNR, SSIM, and LPIPS [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Effectively handles corrupt stereo depth caused by fuzzy pixels and specular highlights, preventing abrupt artifacts in reconstruction [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that early training outputs are smoother and more accurate for corrupt regions, which may not hold for all scenes or noise distributions [S1].
-- **When to Use:** When stereo depth priors are noisy due to specularities, motion blur, or textureless regions, and direct supervision leads to overfitting artifacts [S1].
-- **When Not to Use:** When high-quality, clean depth priors are available, or when the scene geometry is simple enough that standard depth supervision suffices [S1].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- The method adds a one-time (or periodic) overhead per refinement step to compute residuals and patch depth. This is $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of pixels in the depth map [S1].
+- Memory overhead is minimal, requiring storage for the residual map and mask [S1].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- Evaluated on robotic surgery stereo videos with 807 frames across 6 cases [S1].
+- Improves reconstruction quality by reducing abrupt artifacts caused by corrupt depth [S1].
+- Specific quantitative metrics (PSNR, SSIM, LPIPS) are reported in the source but exact values for this method alone are not isolated in the provided notes [S1].
+
+**Scaling:**
+- The method scales linearly with image resolution [S1].
+- The choice of $K$ and $\alpha$ affects the trade-off between smoothing and detail preservation [S1].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- **Strengths:** Effectively handles corrupt stereo depth from fuzzy pixels and specular highlights [S1]. Simple to implement [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the underfitting model [S1]. May smooth out valid sharp depth transitions if $\alpha$ is too large [S1].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when stereo depth supervision is noisy or contains outliers due to sensor limitations or scene properties (e.g., specularities) [S1].
+- Not suitable when high-fidelity depth is required without any smoothing [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of neural rendering for stereo 3D reconstruction of deformable tissues in robotic surgery [S1]. The method addresses the challenge of using stereo depth in surgical scenes where specular highlights and fuzzy pixels corrupt the depth estimates [S1]. Preliminary findings indicated that underfitting models at early training stages produce smoother depth results, which can be leveraged to correct corrupt depth [S1]. The method was implemented with a depth refinement iteration count $K=4000$ and threshold $\alpha=0.1$ [S1].
+Originated in the context of neural rendering for stereo 3D reconstruction of deformable tissues in robotic surgery [S1]. The method addresses the challenge of overfitting to corrupt depth in surgical scenes, where fuzzy pixels and specular highlights are common [S1]. Preliminary findings showed that early-stage models produce smoother depth due to averaging of learned colors and occupancy, which was leveraged to identify and patch corrupt depth [S1].
 
